1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,566
Aider brings AI pair programming into your terminal.

2
00:00:02,716 --> 00:00:06,187
It helps you start new work or extend an existing codebase.

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:10,020
Aider maps your codebase so it can work in larger projects.

4
00:00:10,170 --> 00:00:16,347
It connects to cloud and local LLMs, and it accepts images, web pages, and voice.

5
00:00:16,867 --> 00:00:21,406
The workflow stays grounded in git, with automatic commits and sensible messages.

6
00:00:21,556 --> 00:00:25,213
That makes it easy to diff, manage, and undo AI changes.

7
00:00:25,733 --> 00:00:30,465
Getting started is a short shell session: install aider-install, change into your

8
00:00:30,465 --> 00:00:33,268
project, then launch aider with a model and key.

9
00:00:33,418 --> 00:00:39,003
The README shows DeepSeek, Claude 3.7 Sonnet, and o3-mini as examples.

10
00:00:39,533 --> 00:00:45,187
The benchmark report tracks pass rates, well formed responses, runtime, and cost.

11
00:00:45,337 --> 00:00:49,524
Those numbers come from the benchmark harness that evaluates edits against unit

12
00:00:49,524 --> 00:00:49,842
tests.

13
00:00:50,367 --> 00:00:54,932
To reproduce a run, clone the repo, build the docker container, and launch the

14
00:00:54,932 --> 00:00:56,102
benchmark inside it.

15
00:00:56,252 --> 00:01:00,757
Then install the development build and run benchmark.py with your model and edit

16
00:01:00,757 --> 00:01:01,151
format.

17
00:01:01,667 --> 00:01:04,070
Clone Aider and try it on your own codebase.

18
00:01:04,220 --> 00:01:08,005
If you want measurable results, the benchmark harness is ready too.
